Shoals.Curves.yield_curve_from_pillars: pillar times must be strictly increasing: index 2 has time 2.0, which does not exceed time 2.0 at index 1

The rule is strictly increasing, not non-decreasing: two pillars at one time
carry two different rates at one date, so no curve reads both, and the
interpolator's bracket width there is zero. This is the negative-parity case
for the `gt` in the guard -- a `gte` would accept it and the four unsorted
cases above would still pass. `bootstrap_multi` already rejects a duplicate
tenor for the same reason (`bootstrap_duplicate_tenor_neg.ch`).
